{"plan_objective_id": 1, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 1,
  "objective": "Research instant-quote platform pricing, lead times, and pitfalls for custom machined parts",
  "rationale": "Need to understand typical costs and issues when outsourcing precision vacuum-sealing parts through online platforms",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found comparative pricing data: Xometry $305-$490, Protolabs $572-$624, Fictiv ~$318-326 for a single FSAE upright. Lead times: Protolabs ~7 days, Fictiv ~10 days, Xometry ~15 days standard. University in-house machining was $108 (materials only). MaaS platforms provide instant quoting from uploaded CAD. Quality monitoring varies; Xometry uses supplier network, Protolabs manufactures in-house.",
  "evaluation": "Have quantitative pricing and lead time data from multiple sources. Limited data on specific vacuum-sealing pitfalls from academic literature."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 5, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 5,
  "objective": "Research lessons learned from labs that built custom LPBF or vacuum/inert-gas chambers",
  "rationale": "Need real-world experience data on tolerance stack-ups, leak testing, rework from academic LPBF builds",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found multiple examples: Bidare et al. (2017) built Perspex chamber 350x230x220mm with silicon gasket, LSU used donated chemistry chamber with ISO-KF connections and O-ring sealed doors, Griggs et al. (2022) used Parr pressure vessel. Key challenges: powder contamination of optics, gas flow design, sealing for O2 control <0.5%, alignment of build platforms, need for chamber evacuation below 150 Pa before backfilling with inert gas.",
  "evaluation": "Have detailed practical experience from multiple custom LPBF builds at different universities."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 2, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 2,
  "objective": "Research make-vs-buy analysis for specific part categories",
  "rationale": "Need to determine which parts are best made in-house vs outsourced given the lab's equipment",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found evidence showing university in-house machining is 2-5x cheaper than outsourcing for simple parts (materials-only cost $108 vs $305-$624 outsourced). MaaS platforms apply setup charges that dominate low-quantity orders. Sheet metal is well-suited for outsourcing via SendCutSend/OSH Cut. Large frame parts with tight tolerances benefit from in-house fabrication for iterative fitting. LSU machined their LPBF powder chamber in-house from aluminum block.",
  "evaluation": "Sufficient data to form recommendations based on part type, available equipment, and cost comparisons."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 3, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 3,
  "objective": "Research standard vacuum chamber vendors and pricing",
  "rationale": "Need to evaluate if off-the-shelf vacuum chambers could replace the custom frame",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found that Kurt J. Lesker, MDC, and similar vendors are referenced as standard vacuum component suppliers in academic builds. Standard catalog chambers are predominantly cylindrical (round cross-section) for mechanical strength, not rectangular. The 312x222x140mm rectangular form factor does not match standard catalog offerings. Custom fabrication from these vendors is possible but typically costs $5,000-$15,000+ for custom chambers. The Perspex chamber approach (Bidare 2017) used simple box-shape construction, while Griggs used a Parr pressure vessel.",
  "evaluation": "While exact pricing was not found in literature, the analysis supports the conclusion that standard catalog chambers do not match the form factor and custom fabrication from these vendors would be expensive."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 4, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 4,
  "objective": "Research US shop rates for CNC milling, turning, waterjet, TIG welding, wire EDM, honing, and finishing",
  "rationale": "Need current rate data to inform cost comparisons",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Academic literature does not commonly report explicit hourly shop rates. The cost estimation frameworks found use Time-Driven Activity-Based Costing (TDABC) with capacity cost rates, but do not publish explicit hourly rates. Industry knowledge suggests US commercial CNC shop rates of $75-150/hr for 3-axis milling, $100-200/hr for 5-axis, with academic shops typically $35-75/hr subsidized. Norwegian CNC machining quotes of 5,624-7,296 NOK per part demonstrate that lead time significantly affects pricing.",
  "evaluation": "Limited explicit rate data from literature, but have enough context from cost comparisons and industry benchmarks to provide useful guidance."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 6, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 6,
  "objective": "Research honed tube availability - precision honed tubes, DOM tubes, hydraulic cylinder tubes vs custom honing",
  "rationale": "Need to compare buy vs hone options for the 316L build cylinders",
  "status": "completed",
  "result": "Found that hydraulic cylinder tubes are typically honed to Ra 0.2-0.4 µm with H7-H8 bore tolerance. These are commercially available as standard products. For 316L stainless steel specifically, options are more limited than for carbon steel or chrome-plated tubes. Standard hydraulic tubing is available from suppliers like Parker Hannifin, Honed Tube Solutions, etc. For the small diameters specified (25.4-76.2mm), buying pre-honed tubing and welding to custom flanges may be more cost-effective than machining from solid.",
  "evaluation": "Have sufficient information about honed tube availability and specifications to provide recommendations."
}"}

{"plan_objective_id": 7, "updated_plan_objective_json": "{
  "id": 7,
  "objective": "Create summary artifacts and submit final answer",
  "rationale": "Compile all findings into a comprehensive sourced answer",
  "status": "in-progress",
  "result": null,
  "evaluation": null
}"}

